### Quest Lines ###

## GT Simple Wireless Network

betterquesting.questline.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.name=§5§lGT Simple Wireless Network
betterquesting.questline.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.desc=§3§lPurpose and operation§r%nGTSWN provides wireless EU transfer for supported GT energy containers, network monitoring and device status management, with ME quantum bridging through AE2.%n%n§3§lChapter route§r%nThe §114§r quests cover monitors, the portable terminal, wireless link covers, network info panels, the ME quantum network and the Device Info Terminal. Keep the relevant chunks loaded; ME features also require valid AE power and channel management.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.name=§2§lEverything Under Control
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.desc=§3§lPurpose and operation§r%nGTSWN provides wireless EU transfer, wireless network monitoring, network info panels, ME quantum bridging and device status management. Wireless energy features serve supported GT energy containers; ME quantum features require AE2.%n%n§3§lChapter route§r%nThis chapter contains §114§r quests: start with the Wireless Energy Monitor and Portable Wireless Network Monitor, then configure the Link Terminal and its energy and power covers, and continue to info panels, the ME Quantum Terminal and the Device Info Terminal. Tick this quest after reading.%n%n§3§lOperating requirements§r%nThe relevant machines, panels and quantum anchors need their chunks loaded. ME bridging also requires channel capacity management and AE network power; material and power requirements vary by feature.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAg.name=§3§lWatching the Watts
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAg.desc=§3§lPurpose and operation§r%nThe Wireless Energy Monitor reads wireless grid energy and power changes. It accepts and outputs no EU, stores no EU internally, and needs no power supply. Data updates every §1100 ticks (5 seconds)§r over a §1300-second§r history window.%n%n§3§lPreparation and quest§r%nThe crafting-table recipe requires §14§r LV Sensors, §12§r Steel Plates, §11§r LV Machine Hull and §12§r Steel Screws. This quest detects §11§r monitor and §2does not consume it§r.%n%n§3§lChunk loading§r%nKeep the monitor chunk loaded. If the gap between updates exceeds §1200 ticks (10 seconds)§r, the monitor clears trend samples and delays redstone checks by §1600 ticks (30 seconds)§r. Shorter gaps retain the samples. This handles unloading or gaps without updates, rather than loss of power.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAw.name=§3§lSmart Grid
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAw.desc=§3§lPreparation and use§r%nPlace the Wireless Energy Monitor and right-click to open its interface; no power connection is needed. The measurement anchor selects wireless stored EU, §15-second§r average EU/t or instantaneous EU/t. Parameters accept direct entry and scientific notation, such as §11.5E6§r.%n%n§3§lRedstone control§r%nThe redstone button offers §15§r modes: Off; output above Param1; output below Param1; High-Hysteresis (enable above Param1, disable below Param2); and Low-Hysteresis (enable below Param2, disable above Param1). Signal strength is §115§r, with checks every §12 ticks§r. Data updates every §1100 ticks§r over a §1300-second§r history window.%n%n§3§lQuest requirements§r%nObtain §11§r comparator and tick the quest after understanding the measurement target and threshold directions. The comparator is §2detected without consumption§r; the quest does not automatically check the redstone circuit or parameter settings.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABA.name=§d§lPocket-Sized Oversight
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABA.desc=§3§lMonitoring and binding§r%nThe first right-click binds the Portable Wireless Network Monitor to the current player. Later right-clicks cycle the HUD through Off, Normal and Scientific; sneak-right-click rebinds it to the current player. In inventory or a Baubles slot, it displays the bound grid's EU, instantaneous and average EU/t, and power tier. This quest requires crafting §11§r monitor and ticking the confirmation.%n%n§3§lBattery installation and charging§r%nShapelessly craft the monitor with a compatible GT/IC2 rechargeable battery to install it; the item remains the same monitor. Battery capacity and voltage tier determine its buffer and charging rate, and the new battery's charge enters the buffer. While carried in inventory or a Baubles slot with a partly empty buffer, it requests a full refill every §1200 ticks (10 seconds)§r. The grid pays supplied EU × (§11§r + downlink loss fraction).%n%n§3§lTool charging and battery replacement§r%nCompatible electric tools in inventory, armor and Baubles slots charge simultaneously at §11A × battery voltage§r per tool, limited by the remaining buffer. Charge updates preserve held-tool use and mining. Shapelessly craft with a new battery to replace the installed one: the new battery retains its own charge, while the old battery receives the remaining monitor buffer and returns to inventory, or drops if inventory is full. Tips appear on first acquisition and after §110§r and §120 minutes§r of continued possession, for §13§r tips in total.%n%n§3§lCharging status and settings§r%nA battery-equipped monitor adds a charging status line: Buffer empty, Standby, Charging and Charging complete. After §11 second§r without new charging demand, Charging complete appears for about §15 seconds§r before Standby; new demand resumes Charging immediately. Use /gtswn hud charge on/off to toggle the charging line; the eu, instant and average subcommands control the energy, instantaneous and average lines. All four lines have configuration options, and commands save their corresponding settings.%n%n§3§lBattery removal and recipe lookup§r%nShapelessly craft a battery-equipped monitor with a GT5U crowbar to remove its battery. The old battery retains the remaining buffer charge and returns to inventory, or drops if inventory is full. NEI provides §12§r crafting-table examples: installing a GT small lithium battery and removing it with a GT5U crowbar. Installation and replacement accept compatible batteries beyond that example. An equipped monitor shows its buffer through GT/IC2 energy tooltips and a charge bar.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABQ.name=§6§lA Connection Across Dimensions
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABQ.desc=§3§lPurpose and quest§r%nThe Wireless Network Link Terminal installs Link Anchor covers on supported GT energy containers. Energy covers draw from the wireless grid, while power covers upload machine output. This quest requires crafting §11§r Link Terminal.%n%n§3§lModes and interaction§r%nShift-right-click the terminal to switch Energy and Power modes; its texture indicates the active mode. The default grid interaction interval is §1600 ticks (30 seconds)§r, with §115%%§r downlink loss and §10%%§r uplink loss. Configure InteractionRateTicks and the losses in config/gtswn/gtswn_network.cfg. §cShorter intervals increase interaction frequency and processing overhead.§r%n%n§3§lBuffers and operating requirements§r%nEnergy covers use a finite buffer with a default capacity of §1V × A × 800 EU§r; power covers use Long.MAX_VALUE as their buffer limit. The wireless EU grid supports cross-dimension transfer, while participating machines still need their chunks loaded.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABg.name=§1§lLight Up Every Corner
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABg.desc=§3§lPreparation and use§r%nSwitch the Link Terminal to Energy mode and right-click a cover-compatible position on a supported GT base energy container. Energy mode shows a yellow §13 × 3§r guide grid. Binding a laser hatch consumes §11§r Laser Vacuum Pipe. Tick this quest after understanding the binding procedure.%n%n§3§lBuffer and power delivery§r%nThe cover injects up to §1V × A EU§r per tick and refills from the wireless grid at each interaction interval. Capacity is V × A × (interaction ticks + redundancy ticks) EU. The defaults are §1600§r interaction ticks and §1200§r redundancy ticks, giving §1V × A × 800 EU§r. Default downlink loss is §115%%§r; it and the interaction interval are configurable in config/gtswn/gtswn_network.cfg.%n%n§3§lRemoval and remaining energy§r%nWhen the cover is removed, its remaining buffer is credited to the wireless grid at (§11§r − uplink loss fraction). §cThe remainder returns to the grid rather than as a player item; keep the machine chunk loaded.§r
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABw.name=§5§lGather Every Joule
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAABw.desc=§3§lPreparation and use§r%nShift-right-click the Link Terminal to select Power mode, then right-click a supported machine with readable energy output to install the power cover. Power mode shows a purple §13 × 3§r guide grid. Tick this quest after understanding energy upload.%n%n§3§lUpload and losses§r%nEach tick, the cover draws up to §1V × A EU§r from available machine output into its buffer, then uploads it at each grid interaction. The default interval is §1600 ticks (30 seconds)§r with §10%%§r uplink loss. Its buffer limit is Long.MAX_VALUE. Configure the interval and losses in config/gtswn/gtswn_network.cfg.%n%n§3§lOutput restrictions§r%nThe cover blocks output to physical cables on that face to prevent double counting; it draws no energy from machines without output. The machine chunk must remain loaded for collection and upload.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACA.name=§3§lTrends on a Wall
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACA.desc=§3§lPurpose and quest§r%nThe Network Info Panel displays wireless EU data and real-time changes and history curves for ME items and fluids. This quest requires crafting §11§r panel. EU history is shared by the placing player's UUID; AE monitoring data is recorded separately for each panel position.%n%n§3§lPreparation and use§r%nRight-click with an empty hand to open configuration. ME monitoring requires a connection to the ME network. Right-click with an item or a recognized filled fluid container to bind a chart target or add or remove live monitoring entries, depending on the active tab. Keep the panel chunk loaded.%n%n§3§lHistory recording§r%nHistory offers §18§r time windows: §15 minutes, 1 hour, 8 hours, 24 hours, 7 days, 1 month, 3 months and 1 year§r. Each window retains its own §161§r FIFO data points. Several curve styles are available, with Fritsch–Carlson splines as the default.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACQ.name=§3§lThe Bigger Picture
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACQ.desc=§3§lPurpose and layout§r%nPlace extender panels beside compatible main or extender panels to form a continuous rectangular display. The structure finds adjacent compatible screen blocks and selects the largest filled rectangle containing the main panel. Keep the intended display area continuous and filled.%n%n§3§lQuest and reward§r%nThis quest requires crafting §12§r extender panels and awards §28 additional extender panels§r. The crafting target is not a consumption task; the reward supplies extra items rather than returning materials.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACg.name=§d§lQuantize the ME Network
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACg.desc=§3§lPreparation and quantization§r%nThis quest requires crafting §11§r ME Network Quantum Terminal. Right-click a formed, online controller structure supplied with AE network power. Once its proxy is ready, the connected controller structure is quantized, the terminal binds to its anchor, and channel capacity is recorded.%n%n§3§lConnections and power§r%nUse the bound terminal to place Quantum Nodes and bridge devices into the ME network. The quantized controller filters ordinary connections while retaining controllers in the same structure, energy acceptors or energy components, and Quartz Fibre facing the controller. Maintain a valid AE power path. Shift-right-click air to open the terminal interface and choose from §116§r network colors, with purple as the default. Only the central particles on the terminal itself change color; Quantum Nodes and incorporated devices bound to that network follow its color, helping distinguish multiple networks.%n%n§3§lChannel overload§r%n§cExceeding channel capacity starts a 3-minute countdown; remaining overloaded until it ends detonates the controller structure.§r Returning within capacity cancels the countdown. With AE2 infinite channels enabled, §2channel caps and overload explosions do not apply§r.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACw.name=§d§lCompress the Quantum Ring!
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAACw.desc=§3§lPreparation and use§r%nBind the ME Quantum Terminal to a quantized controller, then right-click the target position to place a Quantum Node. It connects adjacent AE2 devices to the anchor network through a virtual bridge. Tick this quest after understanding placement and bridging.%n%n§3§lChannels and power draw§r%nEach bridge uses a dense connection carrying up to §132§r channels; the node itself consumes no channel. Default idle draw is §110 AE/t§r. §2Virtual bridging supports cross-dimension connections without ordinary cable routing distance limits§r.%n%n§3§lChunk loading and removal§r%n§cThe anchor dimension and chunk must already be loaded; Quantum Nodes do not load their anchor.§r Shift-right-click a node with the Quantum Terminal to remove it.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADA.name=§d§lMind Your Channels
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADA.desc=§3§lChecking channels§r%nShift-right-click air to open the Quantum Terminal interface and check used channels, total capacity, percentage, incorporated-device count and the channels those devices actually use. Press E or Esc to close the interface. The percentage uses the red overload color only when usage exceeds total capacity. Tick this quest after understanding capacity and overload handling.%n%n§3§lCapacity and warnings§r%nController capacity is exposed faces × §132§r channels; shared faces between neighboring controllers are excluded at both ends. Each node bridge carries up to §132§r channels. When a node gains channels and total usage reaches at least §195%%§r without exceeding capacity, it triggers a chat warning; remaining in that range does not continuously repeat the warning.%n%n§3§lOverload and expansion§r%n§cAn overload lasting 3 minutes detonates the entire controller structure; returning within capacity during the countdown cancels the explosion.§r With AE2 infinite channels enabled, §2there is no channel cap or overload explosion§r. Check usage after adding devices, reserve capacity before expansion, and remove idle nodes when necessary.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADQ.name=§6§lThe Machine Ledger
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADQ.desc=§3§lPreparation and quest§r%nThe Device Info Terminal manages supported types of working GT machines. This quest detects §11§r terminal and §2does not consume it§r. Its crafting-table recipe requires §12§r LV Sensors, §13§r Steel Plates, §12§r LV Emitters, §11§r Cover Screen and §11§r Ender Pearl.%n%n§3§lBinding and viewing§r%nRight-click a supported machine to bind it, then right-click air to open the list. Rows display Running, Idle or Stopped status, instantaneous EU/t, a §160§r-point rolling average, position and a readable output snapshot. The output summary is approximated from buffers and does not represent a complete recipe. The §15§r sortable columns are Name, Status, Instantaneous, Average and Position; number formats are Normal, Scientific, metric K/M/G/T/P and GT voltage tiers.%n%n§3§lTeleportation and scanning§r%nThe row teleport button costs §13 XP levels§r by default and can be configured to be free. Ctrl-click unbinds remotely. Shift-right-click air starts a team machine scan in the current dimension with a §15-second§r countdown, checking only loaded chunks. Supported machines newly placed by a player carrying the terminal bind automatically.
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADg.name=§d§lQuantum Incorporation Anchor
betterquesting.quest.R1RXTgAAAAAAAAAAAAAADg.desc=§3§lPreparation and quest§r%nThis quest detects §11§r Quantum Incorporation Anchor and §2does not consume it§r. The LV Assembler recipe uses §11§r Basic Coated Circuit Board, §11§r LV Emitter, §11§r LV Sensor, §12§r Steel Plates, §11§r Ender Pearl and §14§r Fine Copper Wires to produce §116§r anchors in §110 seconds§r at §130 EU/t§r.%n%n§3§lIncorporation and removal§r%nFirst bind the Quantum Terminal to a quantized controller. Hold Alt while aiming at a full AE2 block or GT ME hatch/bus to preview eligibility, then Alt-right-click to incorporate it into the bound ME network, consuming §11§r anchor. Alt-right-click it again to remove incorporation without consuming another anchor; Creative mode consumes none. Cable parts such as panels and buses are unsupported. §cIncorporation hardens the device against explosions and isolates it from external AE cable connections, including Quantum Nodes.§r The original device uses channels normally; the terminal interface shows incorporated-device count and actual channel use.%n%n§3§lLocating members and operation§r%nHold Alt and hold right-click in air for §11 second§r to reveal the Quantum Nodes, incorporated devices and quantized controllers configured with this terminal for §115 seconds§r. Chat confirms that only members configured with this terminal are shown. Repeat the action during that period to reveal all nearby loaded quantum members, with outlines colored by their respective network. §cKeep the anchor and member chunks loaded.§r Each effectively incorporated block adds an actual §150 AE/t§r drain from AE energy storage by default; this additional fee is unaffected by the AE power multiplier. It excludes the original device and channel power costs, which AE calculates normally. Removing incorporation or losing the connection stops this additional fee.
# BQ quests END
